<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Native payload only, no managed API of its own. See src/OpenTok.Transformers.md.

    The classes that use this are already in OpenTok.Net.Android — PublisherKit.VideoTransformer,
    PublisherKit.AudioTransformer, SetVideoTransformers, SetAudioTransformers — and the SDK loads
    the native transformers when one is constructed. Without this package that construction returns
    null and raises 0x0A000006 (MediaTransformerOpenTokTransformersLibraryNotLoaded), at runtime,
    after compiling and linking cleanly.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OpenTokGroupId>com.vonage</OpenTokGroupId>
    <OpenTokArtifact>client-sdk-video-transformers</OpenTokArtifact>
    <!--
      Rides OpenTok's own version line: Vonage publishes this artifact at the same version as the
      SDK it belongs to, so it needs no pin of its own and the repository default applies.
    -->
    <OpenTokNativeVersion>$(OpenTokVersion)</OpenTokNativeVersion>
    <!--
      Bind="false" for the main artifact: its classes.jar is empty (22 bytes — verified against the
      published .aar), so there is no Java surface to project. Everything it carries is native
      payload and .tflite assets.
    -->
    <OpenTokBindArtifact>false</OpenTokBindArtifact>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <Import Project="../OpenTok.Binding.props" />

  <!--
    Two target frameworks, not three — and this is a size fix, not a preference.

    nuget.org rejects a package over 250 MB with HTTP 413, and three copies of the payload came to
    319 MB. The push failed exactly that way before this was here. Dropping net9 and the duplicate
    audio .aar (below) brings it to ~160 MB.

    net9 is the one to drop because it is the one nobody needs an exact match for. NuGet resolves
    the best *compatible* asset folder, so a net9 app takes the net8 copy — verified, not assumed:
    the device tests build against this package at net8, net9 and net10, and all three APKs carry
    every .so and .tflite. That check is .github/workflows/build.yml's transformers-packaging job.

    net8 and net10 are both kept because they are the ends of the range: net8 is the oldest
    supported head and the fallback every other head resolves to, and net10 is the newest, where an
    exact match avoids any question about forward compatibility. Keeping all three would be ~240 MB
    — under the limit, but with 10 MB of headroom, which the next upstream model bump would spend.

    Serving one head's assets to another is safe here in a way it would not be for the binding:
    this package contains *no managed API at all* (see src/OpenTok.Transformers.md), so there is
    nothing target-framework-specific in it. The payload is .so files and .tflite models, byte
    identical in every copy.

    Overriding TargetFrameworks after the import rather than by not setting it: OpenTok.Binding.props
    sets the per-band lists every other project wants, and this is the one project that does not.
    The bands still pair up one-to-one, so build/BuildNugets.sh and merge-packages.py need no
    special case.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks Condition=" '$(OpenTokSdkBand)' == 'net9' ">net8.0-android34.0</TargetFrameworks>
    <TargetFrameworks Condition=" '$(OpenTokSdkBand)' == 'net10' ">net10.0-android36.0</TargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The two ML libraries client-sdk-video-transformers' POM declares. Both are needed, and not only
    to satisfy Java dependency verification:

      * mltransformers-ps16k carries libs/libVonageSelfieSegmentation_android_lib.jar — 159
        MediaPipe classes (com.google.mediapipe.components/framework/glutil) plus
        com.vonage.mltransformers.NativeLib, the loader. The background transformers call into
        those from native code through JNI, so a package shipping only the main .aar links and then
        fails at runtime with NoClassDefFoundError rather than a missing-library error.
      * mltransformersaudionoisesuppression-ps16k is native-only (its own classes.jar is empty),
        and is here because the POM declares it and verification would otherwise fail.

    Bind="false" on both: nothing in OpenTok's public API hands back a MediaPipe type, so these need
    to be on the classpath, not projected into C#. Binding MediaPipe would also mean maintaining a
    metadata file for a surface no consumer of this package calls.

    The main .aar already contains every .so these two carry, byte-for-byte identical, because
    Vonage builds it that way. The duplicate is harmless — the packaging step takes one copy per
    path — and this mirrors exactly what a Gradle consumer of
    `implementation 'com.vonage:client-sdk-video-transformers'` ends up with, which is the
    configuration Vonage actually tests.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <OpenTokMavenArtifact Include="com.vonage:mltransformers-ps16k"
                          Version="$(MlTransformersVersion)"
                          Packaging="aar" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    mltransformersaudionoisesuppression-ps16k is declared by the POM but deliberately NOT shipped,
    and it is worth being precise about why, because "the POM says so" is normally the end of the
    argument.

    Opened and compared, that .aar contains exactly two things: an empty classes.jar (22 bytes) and
    jni/<abi>/libmltransformersaudionoisesuppression.so. No assets, no resources, no Java. And that
    .so is already in client-sdk-video-transformers.aar at the same path, byte for byte — verified
    by SHA-256 on the arm64-v8a copies, which match.

    So it contributes 22.6 MB of duplicate to every copy of this package and nothing else. That was
    tolerable when it was merely wasteful; it stopped being tolerable when the package hit
    nuget.org's 250 MB ceiling.

    Ignored rather than dropped silently: Java dependency verification reads the POM and would
    otherwise fail this build with XA4241 naming an artifact that is, in substance, present.

    mltransformers-ps16k IS still shipped whole, even though ~44 MB of it is the same kind of
    duplicate, because unlike this one it also carries things nothing else has:
    libs/libVonageSelfieSegmentation_android_lib.jar (the MediaPipe classes) and
    assets/selfie_segmentation_gpu.binarypb, which is not in the main .aar under any path. Stripping
    the .so out of it would mean unpacking and repacking an .aar at build time to save 44 MB the
    package no longer needs to save — a real risk to background blur, which cannot be tested without
    a device, for no benefit.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AndroidIgnoredJavaDependency Include="com.vonage:mltransformersaudionoisesuppression-ps16k:$(MlTransformersNoiseSuppressionVersion)" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    No project-level .aar. The .NET 10 Android SDK builds one for a library project and folds the
    native payload of every Bind="false" @(AndroidLibrary) into it — which here means a second,
    complete copy of the jni/ tree: 42 MB of .so files already present in the two .aar files packed
    beside it, and nothing else (10 entries, all jni/). The .NET 9 band does not do this, which is
    why the net10 half of the package was half again the size of the net8 half.

    Harmless in an app — the packaging step takes one copy per path — but this package is nothing
    *but* payload, so a duplicate of it is the single largest thing in the package, and it is the
    difference between ~167 MB and 209 MB against a 250 MB ceiling.

    Safe to turn off because there is nothing else for it to carry: no AndroidResource, no Assets,
    no managed API (see src/OpenTok.Transformers.md). A consumer gets the payload from the two .aar
    files the same way the net8 target framework — which never had a project .aar — already does.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AndroidGenerateLibraryAar>false</AndroidGenerateLibraryAar>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyName>OpenTok.Net.Transformers.Android</AssemblyName>
    <PackageId>OpenTok.Net.Transformers.Android</PackageId>
    <Title>The Vonage Video media transformers for .NET for Android.</Title>
    <Description>The native payload for Vonage's OpenTok media transformers (com.vonage:client-sdk-video-transformers $(OpenTokVersion) and the ML libraries it declares), packaged for .NET for Android. Enables background blur, background replacement and audio noise suppression. Add it alongside OpenTok.Net.Android — it carries no managed API of its own; the classes that turn it on, PublisherKit.VideoTransformer and PublisherKit.AudioTransformer, are in that package. Without this one they return null and raise MediaTransformerOpenTokTransformersLibraryNotLoaded at runtime.</Description>
    <PackageTags>$(PackageTags);transformers;background;blur;noise;suppression</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
